// ============================================================================
// narcissistic-number-check.epher -- three-digit Armstrong numbers
//
// A three-digit number is narcissistic (an Armstrong number) when it
// equals the sum of the cubes of its digits: 153 = 1^3 + 5^3 + 3^3. The
// script checks the four classic three-digit cases with a digit-cube def
// built on floor and mod extraction, then counts how many three-digit
// narcissistic numbers exist below 1000 with a packed while loop
// (guide 1.30). Exactly 153, 370, 371 and 407 qualify.
//
// Source: the narcissistic numbers are sequence A005188 in the OEIS;
// the three-digit case is the classic Armstrong number puzzle of
// recreational number theory (see David Wells, "The Penguin Dictionary
// of Curious and Interesting Numbers"). All results are exact.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/narcissistic-number-check.epher"
// In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/narcissistic-number-check.epher
// Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
//
// There is no knob: the four checks are the point of the script.
// ============================================================================

// ---- the digit-cube sum ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// For a three-digit n = 100a + 10b + c, the hundreds digit is
// mod(floor(n / 100), 10), the tens digit mod(floor(n / 10), 10), and
// the units digit mod(n, 10); the def adds their cubes.
def sumc(n) = mod(floor(n / 100), 10) ^ 3 + mod(floor(n / 10), 10) ^ 3 + mod(n, 10) ^ 3

// ---- the four checks --------------------------------------------------------------------------
for x in {153, 370, 371, 407} do sumc(x) == x
print("153 = 1^3 + 5^3 + 3^3:", sumc(153) == 153)
print("370 = 3^3 + 7^3 + 0^3:", sumc(370) == 370)
print("371 = 3^3 + 7^3 + 1^3:", sumc(371) == 371)
print("407 = 4^3 + 0^3 + 7^3:", sumc(407) == 407)
print("sumc(153):", sumc(153))               // 1 + 125 + 27 = 153

// ---- how many three-digit cases exist -----------------------------------------------------------
// Count the narcissistic numbers from 100 to 999 with a packed loop.
v = {100, 0}
while v[1] < 1000 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if sumc(v[1]) == v[1] then 1 else 0)}
print("three-digit narcissistic numbers:", v[2])    // the four above
// ---- expected output ----
// = {true, true, true, true}
// = 153 = 1^3 + 5^3 + 3^3: true
// = 370 = 3^3 + 7^3 + 0^3: true
// = 371 = 3^3 + 7^3 + 1^3: true
// = 407 = 4^3 + 0^3 + 7^3: true
// = sumc(153): 153
// = {100, 0}
// = three-digit narcissistic numbers: 4
